-- title:
--   Theorem 3.4 — CBA (weights $t^p$) and CBA$^+$ (weights $t^p$ on payoffs, $t^q$ on decisions) solve saddle-point problems with duality gap $O(\kappa L\sqrt{p+1}/\sqrt T)$ and $O(\kappa L(q+1)/(\sqrt{p+
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal X\subset\mathbb R^n$ and $\mathcal Y\subset\mathbb R^m$ be nonempty convex compact sets and $F:\mathcal X\times\mathcal Y\to\mathbb R$ convex in $x$ and concave in $y$. Let $\kappa=\max\{\max\{\|x\|_2,\|y\|_2\}\mid x\in\mathcal X,\ y\in\mathcal Y\}$, and let $L$ bound the norms of the (super)gradients observed in the repeated game framework (bound (4)): in each round $t$ both players choose $x_t\in\mathcal X$, $y_t\in\mathcal Y$, the first player observes $f_t\in\partial_xF(x_t,y_t)$ and the second $g_t\in\partial_yF(x_t,y_t)$, with $\|f_t\|_2,\|g_t\|_2\le L$. Each player runs its own copy of the algorithm on its own set (the second player, who maximizes, on the losses $-g_t$), from arbitrary initial points.
--
--   There are absolute constants $C_1,C_2>0$ such that, for every such problem and every $T\ge1$:
--
--   1. if both players run CBA with weights $\omega_t=t^p$ ($p\ge0$) on both decisions and payoffs, and $(\bar x_T,\bar y_T)=\sum_{t=1}^T\omega_t(x_t,y_t)/S_T$ with $S_T=\sum_{t=1}^T\omega_t$, then
--   $$
--   \max_{y\in\mathcal Y}F(\bar x_T,y)-\min_{x\in\mathcal X}F(x,\bar y_T)\ \le\ C_1\,\frac{\kappa L\sqrt{p+1}}{\sqrt T};
--   $$
--   2. if $p,q\in\mathbb N$ with $q\ge p$, both players run CBA$^+$ with payoff weights $\omega_t=t^p$ and decision weights $\theta_t=t^q$, and $(\bar x_T,\bar y_T)=\sum_{t=1}^T\theta_t(x_t,y_t)/S_T$ with $S_T=\sum_{t=1}^T\theta_t$, then
--   $$
--   \max_{y\in\mathcal Y}F(\bar x_T,y)-\min_{x\in\mathcal X}F(x,\bar y_T)\ \le\ C_2\,\frac{\kappa L(q+1)}{\sqrt{p+1}\sqrt T}.
--   $$
--
--   So the weighted averages of CBA and CBA$^+$ self-play converge to a saddle point at rate $O(1/\sqrt T)$ for every fixed polynomial weighting; larger weights give slightly worse worst-case constants.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Each $O(\cdot)$ of the page is an explicit absolute constant chosen before every other object, so it is uniform in $\mathcal X$, $\mathcal Y$, $F$, the runs, $p$, $q$ and $T$ (the page writes $p$ and $q$ inside the $O$). The runs are the CBA run `cbaX` and the published CBA$^+$ run `ConicBlackwell.cbaPlusX`, with the observed losses; since they are deterministic, quantifying over every pair of loss sequences whose terms are (super)gradients at the played points covers every play of the framework. Subgradients are relative to $\mathcal X$ and $\mathcal Y$ (`BubeckCO.PSGD.IsSubgradient`; the supergradient $g_t$ as a subgradient $-g_t$ of $-F(x_t,\cdot)$). $\kappa$ is `max (kappa X) (kappa Y)`. The max and min of $F$ are suprema and infima of images (bounded under the hypotheses).
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: O(κL√(p+1)/√T) and O(κL(q+1)/(√(p+1)√T)) → ∃ C > 0 (one per statement), chosen before X, Y, F, the runs, p, q and T, with the gap ≤ C times the displayed quantity; reason: the O(·) carry no stated constant and contain p and q, so the uniform reading is the one the page writes; it is the strongest reading and it holds (each player's average regret is at most 2κL times √(Σω_t²)/Σω_t (Theorem 3.1, corrected), resp. (θ_T/ω_T)√(Σω_t²)/Σθ_t (Theorem 3.3); for t^p, t^q these ratios are at most √2·√(p+1)/√T, resp. √2(q+1)/(√(p+1)√T), for every T ≥ 1)
--   - reading: statement 1's exponent p, left unquantified on the page → every real p ≥ 0; reason: statement 2 declares p ∈ ℕ and the paper's comments use p = 0, 1; the bound holds for every real p ≥ 0, so this contains the natural-number reading (negative p is excluded: for p = −1/2 the CBA bound √(Σ1/t)/Σt^{−1/2} is of order √(log T)/√T)
--   - generalization: L = max{L_x, L_y} of (4), a bound on every (super)gradient of F on X × Y → L bounds only the observed ‖f_t‖, ‖g_t‖ for 1 ≤ t ≤ T; reason: the proof uses only the observed ones; every L of (4) is such a bound
--   - generalization: F subdifferentiable on X × Y → only the (super)gradients at the played points are assumed to exist; reason: no other subgradient is used; every subdifferentiable F supplies them
--   - encoding: (x_t), (y_t) generated by the repeated game framework with CBA (resp. CBA+) → x = cbaX X x₀ x₁ ω f and y = cbaX Y y₀ y₁ ω (−g) (resp. ConicBlackwell.cbaPlusX) for loss sequences f, g that are (super)gradients at (x_t, y_t); reason: the runs are deterministic functions of the past losses, so these are exactly the plays of the framework; the second player maximizes ⟨g_t, y⟩, i.e. minimizes the loss −g_t
--   - encoding: the CBA+ run of Algorithm 1 → the published cbaPlusX (u₁ = π_C(ω₁v₁)); reason: the published mission's correction of line 4; the two agree whenever ω₁v₁ ∈ C
--   - encoding: κ, max/min of F, ∂_xF, ∂_yF, ℝⁿ, ℝᵐ, rounds → max (kappa X) (kappa Y) (sSup), sSup/sInf of images, IsSubgradient relative to X and Y, E n, E m, sequences read on Finset.Icc 1 T; reason: sSup is the max on nonempty compact sets; the F-images are nonempty and bounded under the hypotheses; Lean conventions, same instances
--   - encoding: x₁, y₁ (initialization) and x₀, y₀ 'arbitrary' in CHOOSEDECISION → universally quantified parameters in X and Y; reason: the bound holds for every choice
-- source:
--   Grand-Clément & Kroer, Solving Optimization Problems with Blackwell Approachability, arXiv:2202.12277v1 (https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.12277v1), Theorem 3.4, p.10 (proof, Appendix B, pp.23–24; repeated game framework, p.4; (4), p.5)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ApproachRegret_ToOLO_AlgorithmOne
import Definitions.Def_BubeckCO_PSGD_IsSubgradient
import Definitions.Def_ConicBlackwell_cbaPlusX
import Definitions.Def_ConicBlackwell_Supplement_cbaX
open scoped RealInnerProductSpace
open ApproachRegret.ToOLO

namespace ConicBlackwell.Supplement

/-- **Theorem 3.4**, Grand-Clément & Kroer, arXiv:2202.12277v1, p.10 (proof in Appendix B,
pp.23–24).  Let `L = max{L_x, L_y}` (bound (4)) and `κ = max{‖x‖₂, ‖y‖₂ | x ∈ X, y ∈ Y}`.
1. If `(x_t)`, `(y_t)` are generated by the repeated game framework with CBA with weights
   `ω_t = t^p` on both decisions and payoffs, `S_T = Σ_{t=1}^T ω_t` and
   `(x̄_T, ȳ_T) = Σ_{t=1}^T ω_t (x_t, y_t)/S_T`, then
   `max_{y∈Y} F(x̄_T, y) − min_{x∈X} F(x, ȳ_T) = O(κL√(p+1)/√T)`.
2. Let `p, q ∈ ℕ` with `q ≥ p`.  If `(x_t)`, `(y_t)` are generated by the repeated game framework
   with CBA+ with payoff weights `ω_t = t^p` and decision weights `θ_t = t^q`,
   `S_T = Σ_{t=1}^T θ_t` and `(x̄_T, ȳ_T) = Σ_{t=1}^T θ_t (x_t, y_t)/S_T`, then
   `max_{y∈Y} F(x̄_T, y) − min_{x∈X} F(x, ȳ_T) = O(κL(q+1)/(√(p+1)√T))`.

Formalization Note: each `O(·)` is read as "at most an absolute constant `C > 0` times the
displayed quantity", with `C` chosen before every other object (uniform over `X`, `Y`, `F`, the
runs, `p`, `q`, `T`; the page writes `p` and `q` inside the `O`).  The repeated game framework
(p.4): both players choose simultaneously, the first player observes `f_t ∈ ∂_xF(x_t, y_t)`, the
second `g_t ∈ ∂_yF(x_t, y_t)`.  The first player's run is the CBA run `cbaX X x₀ x₁ ω f`
(resp. the published CBA+ run `cbaPlusX X x₀ x₁ ω f`) on the observed losses; the second player,
who maximizes, runs the same algorithm on `Y` with losses `−g_t`.  Since the runs are
deterministic functions of the loss sequences, quantifying over all loss sequences `f`, `g` whose
terms are (super)gradients at the played points covers every play of the framework.
`f_t ∈ ∂_xF(x_t, y_t)` is `BubeckCO.PSGD.IsSubgradient` relative to `X`; `g_t ∈ ∂_yF(x_t, y_t)` is
`IsSubgradient Y (−F(x_t, ·)) y_t (−g_t)`.  `L` bounds the observed (super)gradients for
`1 ≤ t ≤ T`; `κ = max (kappa X) (kappa Y)`.  In statement 1 the exponent `p` is any real `p ≥ 0`
(`t^p` is `Real.rpow`).  The max/min of `F` are `sSup`/`sInf` of images (bounded under these
hypotheses). -/
theorem theorem_3_4 :
    (∃ C : ℝ, 0 < C ∧
      ∀ {n m : ℕ} (X : Set (E n)) (Y : Set (E m)),
        X.Nonempty → IsCompact X → Convex ℝ X →
        Y.Nonempty → IsCompact Y → Convex ℝ Y →
        ∀ (F : E n → E m → ℝ),
          (∀ y' ∈ Y, ConvexOn ℝ X (fun x' => F x' y')) →
          (∀ x' ∈ X, ConcaveOn ℝ Y (fun y' => F x' y')) →
        ∀ (x₀ x₁ : E n) (y₀ y₁ : E m), x₀ ∈ X → x₁ ∈ X → y₀ ∈ Y → y₁ ∈ Y →
        ∀ (p : ℝ), 0 ≤ p →
        ∀ (ω : ℕ → ℝ), ω = (fun t : ℕ => (t : ℝ) ^ p) →
        ∀ (f : ℕ → E n) (g : ℕ → E m) (x : ℕ → E n) (y : ℕ → E m),
          x = cbaX X x₀ x₁ ω f →
          y = cbaX Y y₀ y₁ ω (fun t => -g t) →
        ∀ (L : ℝ) (T : ℕ), 1 ≤ T →
          (∀ t ∈ Finset.Icc 1 T,
            BubeckCO.PSGD.IsSubgradient X (fun x' => F x' (y t)) (x t) (f t)) →
          (∀ t ∈ Finset.Icc 1 T,
            BubeckCO.PSGD.IsSubgradient Y (fun y' => -F (x t) y') (y t) (-g t)) →
          (∀ t ∈ Finset.Icc 1 T, ‖f t‖ ≤ L) →
          (∀ t ∈ Finset.Icc 1 T, ‖g t‖ ≤ L) →
          sSup ((fun y' => F ((∑ t ∈ Finset.Icc 1 T, ω t)⁻¹ •
                  ∑ t ∈ Finset.Icc 1 T, ω t • x t) y') '' Y)
            - sInf ((fun x' => F x' ((∑ t ∈ Finset.Icc 1 T, ω t)⁻¹ •
                  ∑ t ∈ Finset.Icc 1 T, ω t • y t)) '' X) ≤
            C * max (kappa X) (kappa Y) * L * Real.sqrt (p + 1) / Real.sqrt T) ∧
    (∃ C : ℝ, 0 < C ∧
      ∀ {n m : ℕ} (X : Set (E n)) (Y : Set (E m)),
        X.Nonempty → IsCompact X → Convex ℝ X →
        Y.Nonempty → IsCompact Y → Convex ℝ Y →
        ∀ (F : E n → E m → ℝ),
          (∀ y' ∈ Y, ConvexOn ℝ X (fun x' => F x' y')) →
          (∀ x' ∈ X, ConcaveOn ℝ Y (fun y' => F x' y')) →
        ∀ (x₀ x₁ : E n) (y₀ y₁ : E m), x₀ ∈ X → x₁ ∈ X → y₀ ∈ Y → y₁ ∈ Y →
        ∀ (p q : ℕ), p ≤ q →
        ∀ (ω θ : ℕ → ℝ), ω = (fun t : ℕ => (t : ℝ) ^ p) → θ = (fun t : ℕ => (t : ℝ) ^ q) →
        ∀ (f : ℕ → E n) (g : ℕ → E m) (x : ℕ → E n) (y : ℕ → E m),
          x = ConicBlackwell.cbaPlusX X x₀ x₁ ω f →
          y = ConicBlackwell.cbaPlusX Y y₀ y₁ ω (fun t => -g t) →
        ∀ (L : ℝ) (T : ℕ), 1 ≤ T →
          (∀ t ∈ Finset.Icc 1 T,
            BubeckCO.PSGD.IsSubgradient X (fun x' => F x' (y t)) (x t) (f t)) →
          (∀ t ∈ Finset.Icc 1 T,
            BubeckCO.PSGD.IsSubgradient Y (fun y' => -F (x t) y') (y t) (-g t)) →
          (∀ t ∈ Finset.Icc 1 T, ‖f t‖ ≤ L) →
          (∀ t ∈ Finset.Icc 1 T, ‖g t‖ ≤ L) →
          sSup ((fun y' => F ((∑ t ∈ Finset.Icc 1 T, θ t)⁻¹ •
                  ∑ t ∈ Finset.Icc 1 T, θ t • x t) y') '' Y)
            - sInf ((fun x' => F x' ((∑ t ∈ Finset.Icc 1 T, θ t)⁻¹ •
                  ∑ t ∈ Finset.Icc 1 T, θ t • y t)) '' X) ≤
            C * max (kappa X) (kappa Y) * L * ((q : ℝ) + 1)
              / (Real.sqrt ((p : ℝ) + 1) * Real.sqrt T)) := by sorry

end ConicBlackwell.Supplement
